#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060851} :activity/id #uuid "2a764071-28cf-5711-b033-8d2e6a3fee8f" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050668 :topic/id #uuid "5d74b293-aea3-563f-8c4c-f8febb13e3a8" :topic/math-academy-id 267} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060851 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060989 :tutorial/content "To multiply powers of the same base, we simply *add* the exponents. This is called the **product rule for exponents**.\nFor example, in the expression\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}3}^4 \\times {\\color{blue}3}^2\n$$\n\nboth powers have the same base $({\\color{blue}3})$. Therefore, we can simplify this expression by adding the exponents:\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}3}^4 \\times {\\color{blue}3}^2 = {\\color{blue}3}^{4+2} = {\\color{blue}3}^6\n$$\n\n**Note:** The product rule for exponents is really just a quicker alternative to using repeated multiplication, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3^{4} \\cdot 3^{2} &=  \\\\\n3 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 3_(⏟)_(4copies) \\cdot 3 \\cdot 3_(⏟)_(2copies) &=  \\\\\n3 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 3_(⏟)_(6copies) &=  \\\\\n&= 3^{6}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "3baed7a9-d08b-5c41-8d2c-77d4f4daf35d" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8156 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "3f48be73-d211-5343-8cf8-db9420dc3457" :step/math-academy-id 72192 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060852}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060852 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059871 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059422 :question/id #uuid "3625f2a9-3103-551e-b350-b0c83d9b241f" :question/math-academy-id "e-13039"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "997d255f-89af-5563-b10f-eecaae62d8c0" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049714}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060380} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060381}] :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Power Rule for Exponents With Integer Bases"} :step/id #uuid "16cd0938-867d-5ee4-8e29-6f087478cb3f" :step/math-academy-id 72193 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060853}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060853 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059872 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059423 :question/id #uuid "edac9b20-71bf-544e-8e28-833c376097d5" :question/math-academy-id "e-13040"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0aa91f9b-e324-557c-9a6b-fb3389bbeaf8" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060379} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060383}] :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Product Rule for Exponents With Fractional or Decimal Bases"} :step/id #uuid "5b9cd7c8-3ecb-51f1-93ab-7c849fe2f4c7" :step/math-academy-id 72196 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060854}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060854 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060990 :tutorial/content "We can use the product rule for exponents with negative exponents too. For instance, to simplify $3^3\\times 3^{-5}$, we add the exponents:\n\n$$\n3^{3} \\times 3^{-5} = 3^{3-5} = 3^{-2}\n$$\n\nNow, to evaluate $3^{-2}$, we find the reciprocal of the base $(3)$ and then raise that reciprocal to the same power but with the opposite sign:\n\n$$\n3^{-2} = \\left(\\dfrac{1}{3}\\right)^2 =\\dfrac{1}{3} \\times \\dfrac{1}{3} = \\dfrac{1}{9}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "e2ec5a27-43b5-5d18-88cd-f0ef8f6f9a65" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8157 :tutorial/title "The Product Rule With Negative Exponents"} :step/id #uuid "cd4a5018-e0c4-512c-922c-c931da58594d" :step/math-academy-id 72199 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060855}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060855 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059873 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059424 :question/id #uuid "81027692-8570-59a6-a6f9-b31211baa681" :question/math-academy-id "e-13041"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "908d5434-7533-5d33-bc0d-99f4cc9dd44b" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050495}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059954} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059955} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060384}] :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Products with Negative Exponents Using the Product Rule"} :step/id #uuid "03f9acf5-031f-5d89-9fc0-0c1fa58a8938" :step/math-academy-id 72200}] :activity/title "The Product Rule for Exponents" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186059942}]}
